# Rebuild Guide: GoVersion C2 Detector

A step-by-step guide to rebuild the entire codebase from scratch. Each step compiles and runs on its own, so you can verify as you go.

> [!TIP]
> After every step that says **✅ Checkpoint**, run `go build .` to make sure it compiles. This catches typos early.

---

## Step 0: Project Setup

Create an empty folder called `GoVersion` and initialize a Go module.

```powershell
mkdir GoVersion
cd GoVersion
go mod init goversion
```

This creates [go.mod](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/go.mod) — the only thing Go needs to know this is a project.

Also create the [rules.json](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.json) config file now — you'll need it from step 5 onwards:

```json
{
    "suspicious_ports": [6667, 1337, 8080, 9001, 4444, 9999, 50505],
    "known_c2_ips": [
        "149.28.156.183",
        "162.243.103.246",
        "172.232.58.169",
        "172.232.58.237",
        "193.238.227.136",
        "91.230.49.1"
    ],
    "thresholds": {
        "frequency_of_connections": 10,
        "ip_reuse_connections": 3,
        "p2p_unique_peers": 5,
        "packet_size_anomaly_bytes": 1000,
        "small_avg_packet_bytes": 200,
        "min_packets_for_avg": 10,
        "suspicious_port_connection_count": 10,
        "suspicious_port_time_window_sec": 600,
        "behavioral_connection_count": 50,
        "behavioral_time_window_sec": 300
    },
    "weights": {
        "frequency_of_connections": 1,
        "ip_reuse_repeated_connections": 1,
        "p2p_c2_communications": 75,
        "packet_size_anomalies": 75,
        "c2_suspicious_ports": 1,
        "c2_known_proxies": 1,
        "c2_small_packets": 1
    }
}
```

---

## Step 1: models.go — Define Your Data Structures

**Why this first?** Everything else in the project passes around or stores these types. You need to define *what a netflow record looks like* and *what a result looks like* before you can write any logic.

Create [models.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/models.go):

```go
package main

import "time"

// NetflowRecord represents a single netflow record from JSON data.
// The `json:"..."` tags tell Go's JSON decoder which JSON field maps to which struct field.
// For example, `json:"src4_addr"` means the JSON key "src4_addr" fills the Src4Addr field.
type NetflowRecord struct {
	Type        string `json:"type"`
	Proto       int    `json:"proto"`
	TCPFlags    string `json:"tcp_flags"`
	SrcPort     int    `json:"src_port"`
	DstPort     int    `json:"dst_port"`
	InPackets   int64  `json:"in_packets"`
	InBytes     int64  `json:"in_bytes"`
	Src4Addr    string `json:"src4_addr"`
	Dst4Addr    string `json:"dst4_addr"`
	First       string `json:"first"`
	Last        string `json:"last"`
	Received    string `json:"received"`
	IPNextHop   string `json:"ip_next_hop,omitempty"`
	InSrcMAC    string `json:"in_src_mac"`
	OutDstMAC   string `json:"out_dst_mac"`
	ExportSysID int    `json:"export_sysid"`
}

// IPScore is the final output: one entry per suspicious IP.
// TriggeredReasons is a set (map[string]bool) of which detection rules fired.
// RiskFactor is a percentage (0-100) based on weighted scoring.
type IPScore struct {
	IP               string
	TriggeredReasons map[string]bool
	RiskFactor       float64
}

// pairState tracks aggregated state for a specific src→dst IP pair.
// Used by compound rules that need to look at MULTIPLE records between the same two IPs
// (e.g., "did this pair have 10+ connections within 600 seconds on a suspicious port?").
type pairState struct {
	count             int       // how many flows between this pair
	firstSeen         time.Time // earliest timestamp seen
	lastSeen          time.Time // latest timestamp seen
	hasSuspiciousPort bool      // did any flow in this pair use a suspicious port?
	hasSmallPackets   bool      // did any flow in this pair have small avg packet size?
}
```

**What you learned:**
- `json:"field_name"` tags are how Go maps JSON keys → struct fields
- `map[string]bool` is idiomatic Go for a set (lookup is O(1))
- lowercase struct ([pairState](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/models.go#33-40)) = only visible within this package (unexported)
- UPPERCASE struct ([NetflowRecord](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/models.go#6-24)) = exported, could be used by other packages

**✅ Checkpoint:** This won't compile alone (no [main](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/main.go#12-78)), that's fine. Move to step 2.

