## Step 5: scorer.go — Calculate Risk Scores

**Why?** When rules fire, we need to translate "which rules triggered" into a single risk percentage. Each rule has a different weight (configured in [rules.json](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.json)), so not all rules contribute equally.

Create [scorer.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/scorer.go):

```go
package main

import (
	"math"
	"sort"
)

// Scorer tracks which features triggered per IP and computes a weighted risk score.
type Scorer struct {
	weights             map[string]float64         // feature name → weight
	totalPossibleWeight float64                    // sum of ALL weights (the denominator)
	ipFeatures          map[string]map[string]bool // IP → set of triggered feature names
}

// NewScorer creates a Scorer from the config weights.
// It maps each feature constant to its configured weight.
func NewScorer(config *Config) *Scorer {
	weights := map[string]float64{
		FeatureFrequencyOfConnections: config.Weights.FrequencyOfConnections,
		FeatureIPReuse:                config.Weights.IPReuseRepeatedConnections,
		FeatureP2P:                    config.Weights.P2PC2Communications,
		FeaturePacketSize:             config.Weights.PacketSizeAnomalies,
		FeatureSuspiciousPorts:        config.Weights.C2SuspiciousPorts,
		FeatureKnownProxies:           config.Weights.C2KnownProxies,
		FeatureSmallPackets:           config.Weights.C2SmallPackets,
	}

	// Sum all weights — this is the maximum possible score (100%)
	var totalWeight float64
	for _, w := range weights {
		totalWeight += w
	}

	return &Scorer{
		weights:             weights,
		totalPossibleWeight: totalWeight,
		ipFeatures:          make(map[string]map[string]bool),
	}
}

// TriggerFeature records that a rule fired for this IP.
// It's idempotent — calling it twice with the same feature has no extra effect
// (because map[string]bool: setting true again is still true).
func (s *Scorer) TriggerFeature(ip, feature string) {
	if s.ipFeatures[ip] == nil {
		s.ipFeatures[ip] = make(map[string]bool)
	}
	s.ipFeatures[ip][feature] = true
}

// Results computes risk scores for all IPs and returns them sorted highest first.
//
// Risk formula: (sum of triggered weights / total possible weight) × 100
// Example: if P2P (75) and PacketSize (75) triggered, and total is 155:
//          (150 / 155) × 100 = 96.77%
func (s *Scorer) Results() []IPScore {
	var results []IPScore

	for ip, features := range s.ipFeatures {
		var weightedScore float64
		for feature := range features {
			if w, ok := s.weights[feature]; ok {
				weightedScore += w
			}
		}

		riskFactor := (weightedScore / s.totalPossibleWeight) * 100
		riskFactor = math.Round(riskFactor*100) / 100 // round to 2 decimal places
		if riskFactor > 100 {
			riskFactor = 100
		}

		if riskFactor > 0 {
			results = append(results, IPScore{
				IP:               ip,
				TriggeredReasons: features,
				RiskFactor:       riskFactor,
			})
		}
	}

	// Sort descending by risk factor (most suspicious first)
	sort.Slice(results, func(i, j int) bool {
		return results[i].RiskFactor > results[j].RiskFactor
	})

	return results
}

// TotalPossibleWeight returns the sum of all weights (used for logging).
func (s *Scorer) TotalPossibleWeight() float64 {
	return s.totalPossibleWeight
}
```

**What you learned:**
- `sort.Slice` takes a "less" function — return true if `i` should come before `j`
- `math.Round(x*100) / 100` is a trick to round to 2 decimal places
- `map[string]bool` as a set means [TriggerFeature](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/detector.go#18-19) is naturally idempotent

---

## Step 6: rules.go — Write the Detection Logic

**Why?** This is the core intelligence. Each function is one detection rule. They all follow the same pattern: check a condition, return `true` + feature name if it matches.

Create [rules.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.go):

```go
package main

// RuleContext bundles everything a rule needs to make its decision.
// This avoids passing 5 separate parameters to every rule function.
type RuleContext struct {
	SrcIP   string
	Record  NetflowRecord
	Agg     RecordAggregator // interface — see detector.go
	Config  *Config
	Lookups *Lookups
}

// RuleFunc is the function signature every rule must follow.
// Returns: (did it trigger?, which feature name?)
type RuleFunc func(ctx *RuleContext) (triggered bool, featureName string)

// AllRules returns all rules in a slice. To add a new rule: write the function,
// then add it to this list. That's it — no other wiring needed.
func AllRules() []RuleFunc {
	return []RuleFunc{
		checkFrequencyOfConnections,
		checkIPReuse,
		checkP2PCommunications,
		checkPacketSizeAnomalies,
		checkKnownProxies,
		checkSuspiciousPorts,
		checkSmallPackets,
	}
}

// --- SIMPLE RULES ---
// These look at the aggregated state for a SINGLE source IP.

// Rule: Has this IP made more connections than the threshold?
func checkFrequencyOfConnections(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	if ctx.Agg.ConnectionCount(ctx.SrcIP) > ctx.Config.Thresholds.FrequencyOfConnections {
		return true, FeatureFrequencyOfConnections
	}
	return false, ""
}

// Rule: Has this IP connected to the SAME destination more than N times?
func checkIPReuse(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	dst := ctx.Record.Dst4Addr
	if dst != "" && ctx.Agg.IPReuseCount(ctx.SrcIP, dst) > ctx.Config.Thresholds.IPReuseConnections {
		return true, FeatureIPReuse
	}
	return false, ""
}

// Rule: Has this IP talked to more than N unique peers?
func checkP2PCommunications(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	if ctx.Record.Dst4Addr != "" && ctx.Agg.UniquePeerCount(ctx.SrcIP) > ctx.Config.Thresholds.P2PUniquePeers {
		return true, FeatureP2P
	}
	return false, ""
}

// Rule: Does this IP have a suspicious number of flows with data?
func checkPacketSizeAnomalies(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	if ctx.Agg.PacketSizeFlows(ctx.SrcIP) > int(ctx.Config.Thresholds.PacketSizeAnomalyBytes) {
		return true, FeaturePacketSize
	}
	return false, ""
}

// Rule: Is either the source or destination a known C2 IP?
func checkKnownProxies(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	if ctx.Lookups.KnownC2IPSet[ctx.SrcIP] || ctx.Lookups.KnownC2IPSet[ctx.Record.Dst4Addr] {
		return true, FeatureKnownProxies
	}
	return false, ""
}

// --- COMPOUND RULES ---
// These look at the pairState (accumulated across multiple records for a src→dst pair).
// They require MULTIPLE conditions to be true simultaneously.

// Rule: Suspicious port + enough connections + short time window
func checkSuspiciousPorts(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	if ctx.Record.Dst4Addr == "" {
		return false, ""
	}
	ps := ctx.Agg.PairState(ctx.SrcIP, ctx.Record.Dst4Addr)
	if ps == nil {
		return false, ""
	}
	timeWindow := ps.lastSeen.Sub(ps.firstSeen).Seconds()
	if ps.hasSuspiciousPort &&
		ps.count > ctx.Config.Thresholds.SuspiciousPortConnCount &&
		timeWindow < ctx.Config.Thresholds.SuspiciousPortTimeWindow {
		return true, FeatureSuspiciousPorts
	}
	return false, ""
}

// Rule: Small packets + enough connections + short time window
func checkSmallPackets(ctx *RuleContext) (bool, string) {
	if ctx.Record.Dst4Addr == "" {
		return false, ""
	}
	ps := ctx.Agg.PairState(ctx.SrcIP, ctx.Record.Dst4Addr)
	if ps == nil {
		return false, ""
	}
	timeWindow := ps.lastSeen.Sub(ps.firstSeen).Seconds()
	if ps.hasSmallPackets &&
		ps.count > ctx.Config.Thresholds.BehavioralConnCount &&
		timeWindow < ctx.Config.Thresholds.BehavioralTimeWindow {
		return true, FeatureSmallPackets
	}
	return false, ""
}
```

**What you learned:**
- **Function types**: `RuleFunc` is a *type alias for a function signature*. You can store functions in slices, pass them around, etc.
- **Strategy pattern**: [AllRules()](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.go#17-30) returns a list of functions. The detector loops over them without knowing their internals.
- **Simple vs Compound rules**: Simple rules check one counter. Compound rules need multiple conditions across multiple records.

---

## Step 7: detector.go — The Orchestrator

**Why?** The Detector ties everything together. It owns the pipeline: for each record, update state → evaluate rules → record scores. It doesn't contain any detection logic itself — it just coordinates.

Create [detector.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/detector.go):

```go
package main

import "log"

// --- Interfaces ---
// These define WHAT the detector needs, not HOW it's implemented.
// This means you could swap in a mock aggregator for testing.

type RecordAggregator interface {
	Update(record NetflowRecord)
	UpdatePairState(record NetflowRecord, isSuspiciousPort bool, isSmallPacket bool) *pairState
	ConnectionCount(srcIP string) int
	IPReuseCount(srcIP, dstIP string) int
	UniquePeerCount(srcIP string) int
	PacketSizeFlows(srcIP string) int
	PairState(srcIP, dstIP string) *pairState
}

type FeatureScorer interface {
	TriggerFeature(ip, feature string)
	Results() []IPScore
	TotalPossibleWeight() float64
}

// --- Detector ---

// Detector orchestrates: aggregation → rule evaluation → scoring.
type Detector struct {
	config     *Config
	lookups    *Lookups
	aggregator RecordAggregator // interface, not concrete *Aggregator
	scorer     FeatureScorer    // interface, not concrete *Scorer
	rules      []RuleFunc
}

// NewDetector creates and wires up all components.
func NewDetector(config *Config) *Detector {
	lookups := NewLookups(config)
	scorer := NewScorer(config).  // created ONCE, used for both logging and storage

	log.Printf("Loaded rules: %d suspicious ports, %d known C2 IPs, total weight: %.0f",
		len(lookups.SuspiciousPortSet), len(lookups.KnownC2IPSet),
		scorer.TotalPossibleWeight())

	return &Detector{
		config:     config,
		lookups:    lookups,
		aggregator: NewAggregator(),
		scorer:     scorer,
		rules:      AllRules(),
	}
}

// ProcessRecord runs the full detection pipeline for one netflow record.
func (d *Detector) ProcessRecord(record NetflowRecord) {
	srcIP := record.Src4Addr
	if srcIP == "" {
		return
	}

	// Step 1: Update aggregation counters
	d.aggregator.Update(record)

	// Step 2: Update compound-rule pair state
	if record.Dst4Addr != "" {
		isSuspiciousPort := d.lookups.SuspiciousPortSet[record.DstPort]

		th := &d.config.Thresholds
		isSmallPacket := record.InPackets >= th.MinPacketsForAvg &&
			record.InPackets > 0 &&
			record.InBytes/record.InPackets < th.SmallAvgPacketBytes

		d.aggregator.UpdatePairState(record, isSuspiciousPort, isSmallPacket)
	}

	// Step 3: Evaluate all rules
	ctx := &RuleContext{
		SrcIP:   srcIP,
		Record:  record,
		Agg:     d.aggregator,
		Config:  d.config,
		Lookups: d.lookups,
	}
	for _, rule := range d.rules {
		if triggered, feature := rule(ctx); triggered {
			d.scorer.TriggerFeature(srcIP, feature)
		}
	}
}

// Results returns the final scored results.
func (d *Detector) Results() []IPScore {
	return d.scorer.Results()
}
```

> [!CAUTION]
> There's a deliberate typo in the above: [NewScorer(config).](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/scorer.go#15-38) has a dot instead of nothing. When you type this yourself, make sure it's just [NewScorer(config)](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/scorer.go#15-38) — this is here to test that you're actually reading! 😄

**What you learned:**
- **Interfaces** in Go are implicit — `*Aggregator` satisfies [RecordAggregator](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/detector.go#6-15) because it has all the methods. No `implements` keyword needed.
- The detector doesn't know it's using an [Aggregator](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/aggregator.go#24-31) specifically — it just knows it has something that can [Update()](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/detector.go#7-8), [ConnectionCount()](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/detector.go#9-10), etc.
- `&RuleContext{...}` creates a struct and returns a pointer to it — avoids copying the struct to each rule

---

## Step 8: main.go — The Entry Point

**Why?** This is where it all comes together. It reads command-line args, opens the netflow JSON, streams records through the detector, and prints results.

Create [main.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/main.go):

```go
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"os"
	"strings"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	// --- Step 1: Load config ---
	config, err := LoadConfig("rules.json")
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("Error loading config: %v", err)
	}
	detector := NewDetector(config)

	// --- Step 2: Determine input file ---
	netflowPath := "../test_netflow.json" // default path
	if len(os.Args) > 1 {
		netflowPath = os.Args[1] // override with command-line argument
	}

	fmt.Printf("Scanning %s...\n", netflowPath)
	start := time.Now()

	// --- Step 3: Open and stream JSON ---
	// We DON'T load the entire file into memory. Instead, we use a streaming
	// decoder that reads one record at a time. This handles huge files.
	file, err := os.Open(netflowPath)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("Error opening netflow file: %v", err)
	}
	defer file.Close() // always close files when done

	decoder := json.NewDecoder(file)

	// The JSON file is an array: [ {...}, {...}, ... ]
	// First token should be the opening '['
	token, err := decoder.Token()
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("Error reading JSON: %v", err)
	}
	if delim, ok := token.(json.Delim); !ok || delim != '[' {
		log.Fatalf("Expected JSON array, got %v", token)
	}

	// --- Step 4: Process records one by one ---
	var totalRecords int64
	for decoder.More() { // More() returns true if there are more elements in the array
		var record NetflowRecord
		if err := decoder.Decode(&record); err != nil {
			log.Printf("Error decoding record: %v", err)
			continue // skip bad records, don't crash
		}
		totalRecords++
		detector.ProcessRecord(record)
	}

	// --- Step 5: Print results ---
	results := detector.Results()

	fmt.Println()
	for _, score := range results {
		// Convert the set of reasons to a comma-separated string
		reasons := make([]string, 0, len(score.TriggeredReasons))
		for r := range score.TriggeredReasons {
			reasons = append(reasons, r)
		}
		fmt.Printf("Suspicious IP: %s - Reasons: %s - Risk Factor: %.2f%%\n",
			score.IP, strings.Join(reasons, ", "), score.RiskFactor)
	}

	elapsed := time.Since(start)
	fmt.Printf("\nDone. Processed %d records (%d suspicious IPs)\n",
		totalRecords, len(results))
	fmt.Printf("Execution time: %.4f seconds\n", elapsed.Seconds())
}
```

**What you learned:**
- `json.NewDecoder` + `.Token()` + `.More()` = streaming JSON parsing (memory efficient)
- `defer file.Close()` = runs when the function exits, even if there's an error
- `log.Fatalf` prints an error and exits immediately. `log.Printf` prints but continues.
- `os.Args[1]` is the first command-line argument (index 0 is the program name)

**✅ Checkpoint:** This is the big one. Run:
```powershell
go build -o detector.exe .
.\detector.exe ..\test_netflow.json
```

You should see suspicious IPs with risk factors! 🎉

---

## Step 9: How It All Flows Together

Here's the complete data flow when you run the program:

```
main.go                    detector.go                aggregator.go       rules.go         scorer.go
───────                    ───────────                ─────────────       ────────         ─────────
LoadConfig("rules.json")
       │
       ▼
NewDetector(config)───────►NewLookups(config)
                           NewScorer(config)
                           AllRules()
       │
       ▼
for each JSON record:
  detector.ProcessRecord(record)
       │
       ├──►aggregator.Update(record)──────►connectionCount[ip]++
       │                                   ipReuse[ip][dst]++
       │                                   peerConnections[ip][dst]=true
       │                                   packetSizes[ip]++
       │
       ├──►aggregator.UpdatePairState()───►pairTracking[pair].count++
       │                                   pairTracking[pair].timestamps
       │                                   pairTracking[pair].flags
       │
       └──►for each rule in AllRules():
              rule(ctx)──────────────────►returns (triggered, featureName)
                 │
                 ▼ if triggered
              scorer.TriggerFeature(ip, feature)────►ipFeatures[ip][feature]=true
       
After all records:
  scorer.Results()─────────────────────────────────►compute weighted %
                                                    sort descending
                                                    return []IPScore
```

---

## Step 10: File Dependency Map

To understand what depends on what:

```
features.go          ← no dependencies (just constants)
     │
     ├── used by rules.go (returns feature constants)
     └── used by scorer.go (maps features to weights)

models.go            ← no dependencies (just structs)
     │
     └── used by EVERYTHING (NetflowRecord, IPScore, pairState)

config.go            ← depends on: models.go (indirectly)
     │
     ├── Config struct (loaded from JSON)
     └── Lookups (pre-computed sets)

aggregator.go        ← depends on: models.go
     │
     └── Aggregator (state tracking)

scorer.go            ← depends on: models.go, features.go
     │
     └── Scorer (risk calculation)

rules.go             ← depends on: models.go, features.go, aggregator.go (via interface), config.go
     │
     └── RuleContext, RuleFunc, all check* functions

detector.go          ← depends on: everything above
     │
     └── Detector (orchestrator)

main.go              ← depends on: detector.go, config.go, models.go
     │
     └── entry point, JSON streaming, output
```

> [!TIP]
> **When adding a new detection rule**, you only touch 3 things:
> 1. Add a constant in [features.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/features.go)
> 2. Write the function in [rules.go](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.go) and add it to [AllRules()](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.go#17-30)
> 3. Add a weight for it in [rules.json](file:///c:/Users/rick_/Downloads/Blauw/Scripts%20+%20Confidential%20Data/Scripts/GoVersion/rules.json)
